              ":question/id": "674e7733-7a8e-5804-a867-82c74c583dcc",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-4687",
              ":question/problem": "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\n\t\\right].\n$$\n\n",
              ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 0 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}1} & 1 & 1\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} & 0 & -2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 7\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 0 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & 0\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}1}} & {\\color{black}0} & 1 \\\\ \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}1}} & {\\color{red}1} & {\\color{red}1} & 1\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "f5d1d859-0b86-5507-a7bf-3d805a85adf3",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"
          },
